ne}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 865, 'prompt_tokens': 2463, 'total_tokens': 3328, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd1c-8307-7cf3-b7e9-cc5d2a7f50c5-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': 'up{job="user-service"}'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-80c9d2417a9c8fdf', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2463, 'output_tokens': 865, 'total_tokens': 3328}), ToolMessage(content='("{\'resultType\': \'vector\', \'result\': []}", 12)', id='db7e07bd-0447-4873-9394-58b1f4c1432a', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-80c9d2417a9c8fdf'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='f4b7dcc8-9faf-41b7-8109-7934019c5656'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose a social network application composed of many microservices. The initial step was to get the list of services using get_services, but the response from get_metrics for the user-service showed no results, which might indicate that the user-service isn\'t running or there\'s an issue with the metric collection.\n\nWait, the user first called get_services to list all services in the cluster. Then, they tried to check the metrics for user-service with get_metrics, but the result was an empty vector. That suggests that either the user-service isn\'t up, or the Prometheus isn\'t picking up its metrics. But since the user is in the social-network namespace, maybe they need to check the actual pods and deployments to see if the service is running.\n\nSo the next logical step is to use the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool to get the list of pods and deployments in the social-network namespace. This will help verify if the user-service pod is actually running or if there are any deployment issues. By checking the pods, they can see if there are any crashes or restarts, and look at the deployment status to ensure the correct number of replicas are running. If the user-service isn\'t listed, that\'s a clear sign of a problem. If it is listed but in a crash loop, that points to a different issue. This step is crucial to confirm the current state of the service before diving deeper into metrics or traces.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pods -n social-network"  \nTo verify if the user-service pod is running, I need to check the current state of pods in the social-network namespace. This will confirm whether the service is deployed and operational, or if there are issues like crashes, restarts, or deployment failures. This is a critical first step to validate the service\'s existence and health before further investigation.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=387, prompt_tokens=1661, total_tokens=2048, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd1c-b068-7150-b658-e0186fc923eb-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1661, 'output_tokens': 387, 'total_tokens': 2048}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ab2dc363-0af7-4358-b21f-4a8b302387e6')]]
