ternatively, using \'kubectl describe pod\' for any existing pods, but there are none. \n\nAnother tool to use is \'kubectl describe pod\' for any pods related to user-service, but since there are none, maybe check the events in the deployment. Wait, the deployment\'s events show that it tried to scale up to 1 but then scaled down to 0. That suggests that the pod creation failed, causing the deployment to scale down. \n\nSo the next step is to check the logs of the user-service pod if it was ever created. But since it\'s not listed, maybe the pod was created but immediately failed. However, in the \'get pods\' output, there\'s no user-service pod. So perhaps the deployment is not creating the pod at all. \n\nAlternatively, there might be a problem with the image pull. Let me check the image name again. The image is \'docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest\'. Maybe the registry is unreachable, or the image doesn\'t exist. To confirm, I can try pulling the image manually or check the image pull logs. But since I can\'t run commands outside kubectl, I need to use the available tools. \n\nWait, the \'get_metrics\' tool could be used to check if the user-service is up. Earlier, the query \'up{job="user-service"}\' returned an empty result, which indicates that the service is not running. So the metrics are not being collected, which aligns with the deployment not having any pods. \n\nAnother approach: check the pod\'s status using \'kubectl describe pod\' for any existing pods, but since there are none, maybe the issue is with the deployment configuration. The deployment\'s pod template might have incorrect environment variables or command parameters. \n\nAlternatively, the deployment might have a misconfigured selector or labels, preventing the pods from being recognized. The deployment\'s selector is \'service=user-service\', and the pod\'s labels include \'service=user-service\'. That seems correct. \n\nWait, the deployment\'s pod template has a container with the command \'UserService\'. Maybe the entry point or command is incorrect, causing the container to fail to start. But without seeing the logs, it\'s hard to tell. \n\nSince the user-service pod isn\'t listed, the next step is to check the logs of the deployment\'s rollout or any related events. However, the available tools don\'t include a direct way to get logs. The \'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\' can be used to get logs if the pod exists. But since the pod isn\'t there, maybe the logs are not available. \n\nAlternatively, check the ConfigMap \'user-service\' for any errors. The ConfigMap is mounted as \'user-service-config\'. If the ConfigMap is missing or has incorrect data, the container might fail. So using \'kubectl describe configmap user-service -n social-network\' could help. \n\nBut the user hasn\'t used that tool yet. So the next step is to check the ConfigMap. However, the available tools don\'t include a direct way to get ConfigMap details. Wait, the \'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\' can be used with \'kubectl get configmap\' or \'kubectl describe configmap\'. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to check the ConfigMap \'user-service\' in the social-network namespace. If that\'s missing or has issues, it could be the root cause. Alternatively, check the image pull logs. \n\nBut given the previous steps, the most likely issue is the image pull failure. So, using \'kubectl describe pod\' for any existing pods (but there are none) or checking the image. Alternatively, check the deployment\'s events again. \n\nWait, the deployment\'s events show that it scaled up to 1 and then scaled down to 0. That suggests that the pod was created but then failed, leading to the deployment scaling down. So the pod might have been created but immediately failed. However, in the \'get pods\' command, there\'s no user-service pod. That\'s confusing. \n\nMaybe the pod was created but then terminated due to an error. In that case, the logs would show the reason. But since the pod isn\'t listed, perhaps the deployment is not creating it. \n\nAnother possibility: the deployment has a misconfigured replica count. The deployment\'s replicas are set to 0, but the user-service deployment\'s replicas are 0. Wait, in the \'get deployments\' output, the user-service deployment has 0 desired replicas. That\'s the problem. The deployment is set to 0 replicas, so no pods are created. \n\nWait, looking back at the \'get deployments\' output, the user-service deployment has 0/0 replicas. That means the desired replicas are 0. So the deployment is not scheduled to run any pods. That\'s the root cause. \n\nBut why is the desired replica count 0? Maybe the Helm chart or the deployment configuration was incorrectly set. The user-service deployment might have been updated to 0 replicas, or there\'s a misconfiguration in the deployment spec. \n\nSo the next step is to check the deployment\'s YAML to see why the replica count is 0. Using \'kubectl get deployment user-service -n social-network -o yaml\' would show the spec. But the available tools don\'t include that. However, the \'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\' can be used with \'kubectl get deployment user-service -n social-network -o yaml\'. \n\nBut the user hasn\'t done that yet. So the next tool call should be to get the deployment\'s YAML to check the replica count and other configurations. That would confirm if the replica count is indeed 0 and why. \n\nAlternatively, check the deployment\'s history with \'kubectl rollout history deployment/user-service -n social-network\' to see if there were any changes that set the replica count to 0. \n\nBut given the previous steps, the most likely issue is that the deployment\'s replica count is set to 0, which is why no pods are running. So the root cause is the deployment configuration having 0 replicas. \n\nBut how did that happen? Maybe a misconfiguration in the Helm chart or a manual edit. The user needs to check the deployment\'s spec. \n\nSo the next tool call should be to get the deployment\'s YAML to inspect the replica count and other settings. That would confirm the root cause.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get deployment user-service -n social-network -o yaml"  \nThe user-service deployment has 0 replicas, which is the root cause of the issue. To confirm this, I need to inspect the deployment\'s YAML configuration to verify the replica count and identify why it was set to 0. This will reveal if there was a misconfiguration, a failed rollout, or an intentional change that caused the service to stop running.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=1811, prompt_tokens=4261, total_tokens=6072, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd1d-3e87-77c3-8d50-afddc075c392-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4261, 'output_tokens': 1811, 'total_tokens': 6072}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='2a4fa79f-644c-423e-93e4-387c5062d9cc')]]
