eated but then scaled down. Maybe the pod failed to start, causing the deployment to scale it down. To find out why the pod failed, I need to check the logs of the pod. But since the pod isn\'t running, maybe the image is invalid. Let me try to check the image pull status. Alternatively, maybe the container is crashing on startup. Let me try to get the logs of the pod if it exists. Wait, the pod might have been created but immediately failed. Let me check the logs for the pod. But first, I need the pod name. Since the deployment is user-service, the pod name would be something like user-service-559b79c875-... but it\'s not listed. So maybe the pod is in a CrashLoopBackOff state. Let me check the logs for the pod. If the pod exists but is crashing, the logs would show the error. Alternatively, maybe the image isn\'t available. Let me check the image pull status. But how? Maybe using kubectl describe pod, but since the pod isn\'t listed, maybe I need to check the deployment\'s events again. Wait, the deployment\'s status shows that the replica set was created but then scaled down. The event says "Scaled down replica set user-service-559b79c875 to 0 from 1". That suggests that the pod was created but then terminated. Maybe there\'s an error in the pod\'s logs. Let me try to get the logs for the pod. But since the pod isn\'t running, maybe it\'s in a CrashLoopBackOff state. Let me check the logs. If the pod is in CrashLoopBackOff, the logs would show the reason for the crash. So I need to find the pod name. Wait, the deployment\'s template has a hostname of user-service. Maybe the pod name is user-service-... but it\'s not listed. Alternatively, maybe the pod was created but immediately failed. Let me try to get the logs for the pod. If the pod doesn\'t exist, I\'ll get an error. Let me try that.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-0 -n social-network"  \nThe user-service deployment\'s replica set (user-service-559b79c875) was created but scaled down to 0. To determine why the pod failed to start or crashed, I need to check its logs. This will reveal errors like image pull failures, configuration issues, or runtime exceptions that caused the pod to terminate immediately.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 827, 'prompt_tokens': 7665, 'total_tokens': 8492, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd1d-d95b-79a3-a8b1-da3b2e82640f-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7665, 'output_tokens': 827, 'total_tokens': 8492}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='104b48fe-8d27-4a69-a6e5-1f69abfd3bf6'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service deployment has 0 replicas, and the previous steps showed that the ConfigMap exists and seems correctly configured. The next step is to check the logs of the pod that was created but then scaled down. The command "kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-0 -n social-network" is intended to get the logs for that specific pod. However, if the pod has already terminated, the logs might not be available or might show why it failed. \n\nWait, in the previous output, when we ran "kubectl get pods -n social-network", the user-service pod wasn\'t listed. That suggests that the pod might not be running, which aligns with the deployment having 0 replicas. But the deployment\'s status shows that the replica set was created and then scaled down. So, the pod might have started but then crashed, leading to the replica set being scaled down.\n\nBy checking the logs, we can look for any errors that occurred when the pod started. Common issues could be image pull errors