 to re-examine the deployment\'s events and status. This will reveal errors like image pull failures, resource constraints, or configuration issues that prevented pod creation.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 464, 'prompt_tokens': 7915, 'total_tokens': 8379, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd1e-1869-7193-ac8e-99ae52598e81-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7915, 'output_tokens': 464, 'total_tokens': 8379}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='1ac7f872-3df7-49b8-800a-e6825144c37b'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose why the user-service in their social network application isn't running. They've already checked the deployments and found that the user-service deployment has 0 replicas. They tried to get the logs of the pod but it wasn't found. Now, they're asking what the next step should be.\n\nFirst, I need to recall the previous steps. They used kubectl get deployments to check the deployment status, which showed the replicas as 0. Then they checked the ConfigMap, which seemed okay. The next step was to check the logs of the pod, but that pod doesn't exist. So the deployment didn't create any pods, or they were terminated immediately.\n\nThe user tried to describe the deployment again, which might give more info. The deployment's events could show why the pods aren't being created. The error message from the logs command indicates the pod wasn't found, so maybe the deployment never successfully created a pod. \n\nLooking at the deployment's status, the events might have details about why the replicas aren't available. For example, if there's an image pull error, or if the required resources aren't available. The user already checked the deployment's YAML, which shows the replicas set to 0. But why?\n\nThe next logical step is to check the events associated with the deployment. The kubectl describe deployment command includes events that can explain why the deployment isn't scaling up. The user might have already done that, but maybe they need to look again for any recent events. Alternatively, checking the pod's events if they exist, but since the pod wasn't found, maybe the deployment's events are the key.\n\nSo, the best tool here is to describe the deployment again to check the events. The events in the deployment's status can provide clues like image pull errors, resource limits, or other issues preventing pod creation. That's why the next tool call should be to describe the deployment again, focusing on the events section.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe deployment user-service -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-be42a2063365bb8e', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 440, 'prompt_tokens': 8915, 'total_tokens': 9355, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd1e-2ee1-79e1-b919-0aad083fcbbb-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe deployment user-service -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-be42a2063365bb8e', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8915, 'output_tokens': 440, 'total_tokens': 9355}), ToolMessage(content='Name:                   user-service\nNamespace:              social-network\nCreationTimestamp:      Tue, 17 Mar 2026 18:41:28 +0000\nLabels:                 app.kubernetes.io/managed-by=Helm\n                        service=user-service\nAnnotations:            deployment.kubernetes.io/revision: 1\n                        meta.helm.sh/release-name: social-network\n                        meta.helm.sh/release-namespace: social-network\nSelector:               service=user-service\nReplicas:               0 desired | 0 updated | 0 total | 0 available | 0 unavailable\nStrategyType:           RollingUpdate\nMinReadySeconds:        0\nRollingUpdateStrategy:  25% max unavailable, 25% max surge\nPod Template:\n  Labels:  app=user-service\n           service=user-service\n  Containers:\n   user-service:\n    Image:      docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest\n    Port:       9090/TCP\n    Host Port:  0/TCP\n    Command:\n      UserService\n    Environment:  <none>\n    Mounts:\n      /social-network-microservices/config/jaeger-config.yml from user-service-config (rw,path="jaeger-config.yml")\n      /social-network-microservices/config/service-config.json from user-service-config (rw,path="service-config.json")\n  Volumes:\n   user-service-config:\n    Type:          ConfigMap (a volume populated by a ConfigMap)\n    Name:          user-service\n    Optional:      false\n  Node-Selectors:  <none>\n  Tolerations:     <none>\nConditions:\n  Type           Status  Reason\n  ----           ------  ------\n  Available      True    MinimumReplicasAvailable\n  Progressing    True    NewReplicaSetAvailable\nOldReplicaSets:  <none>\nNewReplicaSet:   user-service-559b79c875 (0/0 replicas created)\nEvents:\n  Type    Reason             Age    From                   Message\n  ----    ------             ----   ----                   -------\n  Normal  ScalingReplicaSet  4m16s  deployment-controller  Scaled up replica set user-service-559b79c875 to 1\n  Normal  ScalingReplicaSet  2m39s  deployment-controller  Scaled down replica set user-service-559b79c875 to 0 from 1\n', id='7a2d216e-5d16-4867-a659-ac181599317c', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-be42a2063365bb8e'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='98865518-a139-4fa8-9f5a-fb752a31d25f'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s try to figure out why the user-service is not running. So far, we\'ve checked the deployments and found that the user-service deployment has 0 replicas. The ConfigMap seems okay, and the ConfigMap is being mounted correctly. The logs for the pod weren\'t found, which suggests the pod might not have been created or it failed immediately.\n\nLooking at the deployment\'s events, there\'s a ScalingReplicaSet event where it was scaled up to 1 and then scaled down to 0. That might indicate that the pod was created but then failed, causing the deployment to scale it down. Since the pod wasn\'t found, maybe it didn\'t stay around long enough to be listed. \n\nNext step is to check the pod\'s status. If the pod was created but failed, the events in the deployment might not have enough detail. Maybe we should check the events for the deployment again or look into the pod\'s status. Wait, the user tried to get the pod logs but it wasn\'t found. So the pod might have been terminated quickly. \n\nAnother angle: maybe the image specified in the deployment is incorrect. The image is docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest. Could there be an issue with pulling that image? Let\'s check if the image exists. If the image isn\'t available, the pod would fail to start. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a resource constraint. But the deployment doesn\'t show any resource limits in the spec. The resources section is empty. So maybe the pod is failing due to an image pull error. \n\nTo confirm, we can check the events in the deployment for any image pull errors. The deployment\'s events might have messages about failed pulls. Let me check the events again. The events listed are ScalingReplicaSet up and down. No mention of image pull errors. \n\nWait, maybe the pod was created but immediately crashed. The logs weren\'t available, but maybe the pod\'s status shows why it failed. Since the pod isn\'t present, perhaps we need to check the pod\'s status using kubectl describe pod, but the pod name isn\'t known. \n\nAlternatively, check the pod\'s status in the deployment\'s events. The deployment\'s events don\'t show any errors. Maybe the issue is with the image. Let\'s try to check if the image exists. If the image isn\'t available, the pod can\'t start. \n\nAnother possibility: the ConfigMap might be missing some necessary files. The ConfigMap has jaeger-config.yml and service-config.json. If those files are missing or incorrect, the service might fail to start. But earlier checks showed the ConfigMap exists and has the data. \n\nWait, the user-service deployment\'s pod template has a command: UserService. Maybe the entry point is incorrect. If the command isn\'t valid, the container would fail to start. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the container is crashing due to an error in the application code. Without logs, it\'s hard to tell. But since the pod wasn\'t found, maybe it\'s a transient issue. \n\nWait, the deployment\'s status shows that the replica set was scaled up to 1 and then down to 0. That suggests that the pod was created but then the deployment controller scaled it down. Why would that happen? Maybe the pod failed, and the deployment tried to scale it down. \n\nBut without logs, it\'s hard to know. Maybe the next step is to check the pod\'s status again, but since the pod isn\'t present, perhaps the issue is with the image. Let\'s try to check the image pull status. \n\nAlternatively, check the pod\'s status using kubectl get pods -n social-network again. Wait, the user already did that and didn\'t see the user-service pod. So the pod wasn\'t created. \n\nAnother thought: maybe the deployment is configured to have 0 replicas. Wait, the deployment\'s spec.replicas is set to 0. That\'s the root cause. But why is it set to 0? The user might have accidentally set it, or there\'s a config error. \n\nWait, looking back at the deployment YAML, the replicas are set to 0. That\'s the main issue. The deployment is not trying to create any pods. So the root cause is that the replicas are set to 0. But why? Maybe a misconfiguration in the Helm chart or a manual edit. \n\nBut the user\'s initial check with get_metrics for up{job="user-service"} returned no results, which aligns with the deployment having 0 replicas. So the main issue is that the deployment is not set to run any replicas. \n\nBut why is the replicas set to 0? Maybe the Helm chart was deployed with an incorrect value. The user might need to check the Helm values file. However, the tools available don\'t include checking Helm values directly. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the deployment was updated to 0 replicas by mistake. The user needs to check the deployment\'s spec and correct it. \n\nSo the root cause is that the user-service deployment has replicas set to 0, which means no pods are being created. The reason for this could be a misconfiguration in the deployment\'s spec, possibly due to a Helm chart value or a manual edit. \n\nTherefore, the answer should state that the user-service deployment has 0 replicas, which is the root cause. The next step would be to correct the replicas count, but since the task is to submit the root cause, the answer is that the deployment\'s replicas are set to 0.\n</think>\n\nsubmit_tool  \nThe root cause of the failure is that the `user-service` deployment has `replicas: 0` configured, which means no pods are being created to run the service. This was likely caused by an intentional or accidental misconfiguration in the deployment spec, possibly due to a Helm chart value override or a manual edit. The deployment\'s events show it attempted to scale up but then scaled down to 0, indicating the pods failed to start or were terminated immediately. Without logs or further investigation, the immediate cause is the absence of replicas, preventing the service from running.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=1257, prompt_tokens=8627, total_tokens=9884, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfd1e-47e9-7bf0-9c14-92614937645c-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8627, 'output_tokens': 1257, 'total_tokens': 9884}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='97544302-89e4-40ae-9b12-eb1f7ae8fb30')]]
